Allocation failure returns null instead of panicking.

mlibc's VirtualAllocator::map() asserts that the anonymous mapping
succeeds, so a refused mmap (the kernel's commit limit, ENOMEM) ends the
process with "mlibc: panic" from inside malloc. frg::slab_pool already
handles a map() that returns 0 by returning null, so map() reports the
refusal that way (ld.so, which cannot go on without memory, still
stops). malloc and realloc set errno to ENOMEM when they return null, as
POSIX requires (upstream sets it only in calloc and posix_memalign), and
malloc, realloc and posix_memalign refuse sizes above PTRDIFF_MAX before
the slab pool's rounding to pages can wrap. C++ operator new throws
std::bad_alloc when malloc returns null. Upstream (master, November
2026) still asserts in map().

--- a/options/internal/generic/allocator.cpp	2026-08-02 15:17:51.000000000 -0500
+++ b/options/internal/generic/allocator.cpp	2026-11-26 21:17:57.352653599 -0600
@@ -32,9 +32,17 @@
 // VirtualAllocator
 // --------------------------------------------------------
 
+// A refused mapping is reported as 0: frg::slab_pool then returns null,
+// and malloc and friends fail with ENOMEM. The dynamic linker cannot go
+// on without its memory, so it still stops there.
 uintptr_t VirtualAllocator::map(size_t length) {
 	void *ptr;
+#ifdef MLIBC_BUILDING_RTLD
 	__ensure(!mlibc::sysdep<AnonAllocate>(length, &ptr));
+#else
+	if (mlibc::sysdep<AnonAllocate>(length, &ptr))
+		return 0;
+#endif
 	return (uintptr_t)ptr;
 }
 
